{# The `apache-echarts` showcase — the VIEW. It receives `d` from
   showcase_controller.py and is the only place a macro is called, exactly as
   a report's report.html.j2 is. Each <section> is one state worth seeing.

   This component is the ENGINE, not a wrapper over it, so the states that
   matter are different in kind from bar's or area's. There is no "one series
   vs two" here — there is "what can this reach that the wrappers cannot", and
   "what happens when the spec is wrong".

   BOTH invocation forms appear below, because the macro takes either:
   {% raw %}{% call %}{% endraw %} hands it a body, `option=` hands it a string. #}
{% extends "_showcase.master.html.j2" %}
{% block content %}
    <section>
      <h3>a form no component wraps — candlestick, via a {% raw %}{% call %}{% endraw %} block</h3>
      {% call c.apache_echarts() %}{{ d.candlestick }}{% endcall %}
      <p class="chart-note">OHLC has no wrapper macro, and this is the honest
        reason to reach past them for the engine itself. ECharts reads the data
        as [open, close, low, high].</p>
    </section>
    <hr>
    <section>
      <h3>naming a design colour — via the <code>option=</code> parameter</h3>
      {{ c.apache_echarts(option=d.named_colours) }}
      <p class="chart-note">The target line asks for a specific tone, so the
        spec names one — <code>token:positive</code> — rather than writing a
        hex. The document carries no colour value; the browser resolves it.</p>
    </section>
    <hr>
    <section>
      <h3>a non-default height — the macro emits <code>data-height</code></h3>
      {{ c.apache_echarts(option=d.heatmap, height=d.tall) }}
      <p class="chart-note">Two categorical axes against one measure, on the
        sequential ramp rather than the categorical palette: these buckets are
        degrees of one thing, not unrelated series.</p>
    </section>
    <hr>
    <section>
      <h3>the authoring idiom — literal JSON in the template</h3>
      {# The ONE spec on this page the controller does not validate, and
         deliberately: it is here to show what an author actually types, which
         is the form usage.md documents. Everything else arrives as context, so
         everything else is checked. Keep this one small enough to read whole. #}
      {% call c.apache_echarts(height=280) %}
{
  "xAxis": { "type": "category", "data": ["Q1", "Q2", "Q3", "Q4"] },
  "yAxis": { "type": "value" },
  "series": [{ "type": "bar", "name": "Revenue", "data": [12.4, 13.1, 13.9, 15.2] }]
}
      {% endcall %}
      <p class="chart-note">No tooltip, no legend, no aria in the source — the
        feature adds all three, and only where the author did not.</p>
    </section>
    <hr>
    <section>
      <h3>degradation — a spec that is not valid JSON</h3>
      {{ c.apache_echarts(option=d.broken) }}
      <p class="chart-note">Invalid on purpose: one trailing comma. The card
        never renders, the source shows instead with a red border, and the
        engine is not downloaded on its account. This is the only state of this
        component that cannot be seen any other way.</p>
    </section>
{% endblock %}
